{
  "id": "30483_nakusu",
  "headword": "{亡|な}くす",
  "reading": "なくす",
  "romaji": "nakusu",
  "part_of_speech": "verb (godan)",
  "gloss": "to lose (someone to death); to be bereaved of",
  "definitions": [
    {
      "sense_number": 1,
      "gloss": "to lose (a family member or close person) to death",
      "explanation": "To have someone close die. The subject is the bereaved person, and the object marked by を is the person who died."
    }
  ],
  "examples": [
    {
      "id": "30483_nakusu_ex1",
      "japanese": "{去年|きょねん}{父|ちち}を{亡|な}くした。",
      "english": "I lost my father last year.",
      "sense_numbers": [
        1
      ],
      "has_audio": false,
      "notes": null
    },
    {
      "id": "30483_nakusu_ex2",
      "japanese": "{幼|おさな}い{頃|ころ}に{母|はは}を{亡|な}くしている。",
      "english": "She lost her mother when she was young.",
      "sense_numbers": [
        1
      ],
      "has_audio": false,
      "notes": null
    },
    {
      "id": "30483_nakusu_ex3",
      "japanese": "{戦争|せんそう}で{家族|かぞく}を{亡|な}くした{人|ひと}たちの{話|はなし}を{聞|き}いた。",
      "english": "I listened to the stories of people who lost family members in the war.",
      "sense_numbers": [
        1
      ],
      "has_audio": false,
      "notes": null
    }
  ],
  "notes": "TRANSITIVITY: transitive. The bereaved person is the subject; the person who died is marked with を. The intransitive counterpart is {亡|な}くなる (to pass away), whose subject is the person who died.\n- {妻|つま}を{亡|な}くす: to lose one's wife\n- {妻|つま}が{亡|な}くなる: one's wife passes away\n\nWRITING:\n- {亡|な}くす: to lose a person to death\n- {無|な}くす: to lose an object, to get rid of something\nBoth are read なくす, so the kanji is what distinguishes them in writing.\n\nCOMMON COLLOCATIONS:\n- {親|おや}を{亡|な}くす: to lose a parent\n- {子|こ}を{亡|な}くす: to lose a child\n- {事故|じこ}で{亡|な}くす: to lose someone in an accident\n\nASPECT: {亡|な}くしている describes the resulting state — the person is bereaved now, not that the loss is in progress.",
